\chapter{Terminal output}
The \api{terminal} package provides three small operations: \api{Write},
\api{Clear}, and \api{Color}. These are native functions exposed through a
record, just like the formatting package.

\begin{mullcode}
val T <- G:LoadLib("terminal") or G:DieWith("$E?");
T:Write("status: ready\n");
val red <- T:Color(31, "failure");
G:Assert(G:Len(red) == 16);
\end{mullcode}

\api{Write(text)} passes one string directly to the runtime's stdout callback.
It does not use the formatting package's replaceable main sink. This distinction
lets a host capture all terminal output while a script independently sends
formatted messages to a log collector.

\api{Clear()} writes the ANSI clear-screen and cursor-home sequence.
\api{Color(code, text)} returns a string wrapped in an ANSI SGR sequence and
a reset sequence; it does not print it. The code must be an integer between
0 and 107. The library validates that range, but the meaning of a particular
code depends on the receiving terminal.

The package does not initialize terminal modes, inspect terminal dimensions,
or read keyboard input. Such services can be supplied by the embedding
application as additional native members. Keeping this interface small makes
the default standard library usable with a console, an editor output panel,
or a test callback that simply accumulates bytes.
